answer_en:
The legal test applicable in this case is the test of "external legality" and "internal legality" of the tax assessment procedure, as defined by the Council of State in the "Société Baxter" ruling (CE, 26 October 2001, Société Baxter, n° 198514).

To assess the regularity of the procedure, it is necessary to verify if the following elements are met:

1. The notification of the notice of verification and of the assessment procedure (article L. 47 of the Book of Tax Procedures) ;
2. The motivation of the assessment decision (article L. 48 of the Book of Tax Procedures) ;
3. The respect of the taxpayer's rights, in particular the right to be informed and to present observations (article L. 50 of the Book of Tax Procedures).

Furthermore, to assess the regularity of the method of reconstructing the company's income, it is necessary to verify if the following elements are met:

1. Is the method of reconstruction based on objective and verifiable elements (ruling "Société Sodexho" of 15 May 2002, n° 223422) ;
2. Is the method of reconstruction in conformity with the provisions of the General Tax Code and the administrative instructions (ruling "Société Total" of 12 July 2006, n° 264622).

In the absence of notification of the initial assessment notice, the Bordeaux administrative court of appeal should verify if the conditions of "external legality" are met, in particular if the taxpayer was informed of the assessment procedure and was able to present observations (ruling "M. X" of 21 March 2012, n° 335522).

I am not in a position to provide a more precise answer without knowing the specific details of the case.
